yoyo map <missing path> now fails instead of looking like an empty project, and yoyo def / yoyo outline with no argument now exit 2. It's committed as 73bc8b80 ("Part of #982"). Build, the full cargo test run, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check all pass.

Readings from the built binary, run from `/tmp`:

| Command | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| map /nonexistent_zz | "(no supported source files with symbols found)", exit 0 | ✗ no such path: '/nonexistent_zz' … on stderr, exit 1 (same code as blame on a missing path) |
| def | usage line, exit 0 | same usage line, exit 2 |
| outline | usage text, exit 0 | same usage text, exit 2 |

How it works: I followed the existing pattern. map, def and outline each got a version of their handler that returns an exit code, and dispatch_sub.rs passes that code to the existing exit_if_nonzero. The REPL commands call the same functions and ignore the code. Nothing parses output strings, and there is no second classifier.

What counts as a missing path: /map's path is a prefix filter, so I couldn't just test whether the path exists. That would have broken map src/com, a prefix of real files. A path counts as missing only when the map came back empty, the path isn't on disk (checked from the cwd and from the repo root), and no project file starts with it. That rule is a small pure function, map_missing_path_message, with a table of tests, and the check only runs when the map is empty.

Unchanged, and pinned by tests:
• `map on an existing directory with no supported source files still prints the same line and exits 0. A test checks the exit code with assert_eq on .githooks/, and I also probed an empty /tmp/empty982. A second assert_eq pins the line's text to its constant, so it doesn't prove that line is what gets printed. The printed output was checked by the /tmp/empty982` probe.
• Bare map and map src/com still work as before.
• `def <not found> and outline <no match>` still exit 0. Finding nothing is an empty result, the same call as for grep/find.
• Bare `run` is unchanged.

One extra change: map --bogus already printed an error, and it now exits 2 instead of 0. A function that returns 0 right after printing its own error would have kept the bug, so I took that line. The commit message says so.

Checks:
• **Positive control:** I broke `map_missing_path_message` (so it never reports a missing path), ran the tests and restored it in a single command. Both new `map` tests failed by name, and no marker was left behind.
• **No test crashes:** no in-process test called def or outline with no argument, so the new exits can't kill the test binary.

Docs and issue:
• I updated the exit-codes paragraph in docs/src/usage/commands.md.
• I rewrote #982's body in place with gh issue edit --body-file. These three cases moved to "Already fixed" with the commit hash. I re-probed the "still exit 0" list instead of copying it. Bare run is the only member I re-confirmed. I moved def/outline not-found to "by design".
• I couldn't make undo --all / --last-commit actually fail in a safe place. Outside a repo, or in a new repo with no commits, they just print "nothing to undo" and exit 0. So in the issue that entry is marked unconfirmed, not fixed.
